            <ArticleTitle>Chronic low back pain. What are the treatment options?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>25-30</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Chronic low back pain is a relatively common occurrence in general practice and there is much that the family doctor can do to help.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">An important aspect of the management is to be able to identify red and yellow flag conditions which, if undiagnosed, can become a serious health risk.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Being aware of the possibility that red and yellow flag conditions exist alerts the practitioners to which questions to ask and what the management might be. Patients suffering benign low back pain can be helped to cope by the use of several easily implemented modalities.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
